investment company is not available to Sitio, and it ceases to manage and control Sitio OpCo and its interest in Sitio OpCo is determined to be an “investment security,” Sitio will be required to register as an investment company with the SEC. The ramifications of becoming an investment company, both in terms of the restrictions it would have on Sitio and the cost of compliance, would be significant. For example, in addition to expenses related to initially registering as an investment company, the 1940 Act also imposes various restrictions with regard to Sitio’s ability to enter into affiliated transactions, the diversification of its assets and its ability to borrow money. If Sitio became subject to the 1940 Act at some point in the future, its ability to continue pursuing its business plan would be severely limited as it would be significantly more difficult for Sitio to raise additional capital in a manner that would comply with the requirements of the 1940 Act.
